#{[#edb/ref 17592186049304 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055590} :activity/id #uuid "6a57c066-8362-5ded-8fc6-fe55df2e5373" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055974 :tutorial/content "We add two matrices by adding the corresponding entries. We can only do this if they have the same number of rows and columns, that is, they have the same dimensions.\n\nFor example, consider the matrices\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe see that both $A$ and $B$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices, so we can add them. The sum ${A} + {B}\\,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+1 & 3+0 \\\\ 2+0 & 4+(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, the sum of two matrices is another matrix with the same dimensions." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054545 :question/id #uuid "45832301-e578-578d-8866-147b998a1c22" :question/math-academy-id "e-4374" :question/problem "Calculate $A+B,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-10 & -5+2 \\\\ -2+2 & 3-4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Adding Square Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054649 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054546 :question/id #uuid "fc4a66f3-5180-5ac0-bd4d-830196fa459a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4375" :question/problem "Calculate $A-B,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-0 & -1-(-1) \\\\ -1-2 & 0-(-1) \\\\ 3-(-1) & 2-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Matrices"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055975 :tutorial/content "We can subtract matrices in the same way that we add matrices: we subtract the corresponding entries. Again, we can only do this if they have the same number of rows and columns, that is, they have the same dimensions.\n\nFor example, consider the matrices\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe see that both $A$ and $B$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices, so, we can subtract them. The difference ${A} - {B}\\,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-1 & 3-0 \\\\ 2-0 & 4-(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, the difference of two matrices is another matrix with the same dimensions." :tutorial/title "Subtracting Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054650 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054547 :question/id #uuid "b77816bd-e128-5888-9973-97c51c99f7f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4396" :question/problem "Calculate ${A}+ {B},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+(-2) & 3+(-3) & 1+1 & 0+1 \\\\ 2+1 & 4+(-2) & -1+3 & 2+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Adding Non-Square Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055593}}] :activity/title "Adding and Subtracting Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054891}] [#edb/ref 17592186049162 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060770} :activity/id #uuid "9b6ebd1c-f083-5c13-ba2f-eefcd05878c6" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060969 :tutorial/content "Consider the region below, which represents the inequality $2 < x < 5.$\n\n![](images/fe/feeae0bfa9b29e6f034a3fd33d1e9fbd369840835af2d6588892ee6edc2fa120.png)\n\nWe can represent this inequality using **interval notation** as follows:\n\n$$\n(2,5)\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\phantom{0})$ tell us that the endpoints $x=2$ and $x=5$ are *not* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval does not include the endpoints, we say that it is **open**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (2,5).\n$$\n\nThe symbol $\\in$ means \"lies in\" or \"belongs to.\" So, the above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (open) interval $(2,5).$*\"" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059385 :question/id #uuid "9096883e-6dd0-5dd1-8e9c-176775edadba" :question/math-academy-id "e-10785" :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x \\in (1, 5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only parentheses. So, the endpoints are *not* included. \n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}1, 5{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<      }}\\,\\,      x  \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<      }}\\,\\,      5\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Open Intervals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060970 :tutorial/content "Some intervals may include one parenthesis and one bracket. \n\nFor example, consider the following interval:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{\\big[}}} 1,4 {\\color{red}{\\big)}}\n$$\n\nTo express this interval as an inequality, we note the following:\n\n- The left bracket $\\,\\color{blue}[\\,$ tells us that left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}$ for this endpoint.\n\n- The right parenthesis $\\,\\color{red})\\,$ tells us that the right endpoint $4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{<      }}\\,$ for this endpoint.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq  }}\\,\\,   x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, 4.\n$$\n\nThe interval can be graphed as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a67872e8193c2bcc249a9a9006acb916909099e4babdbe5cdfa6d2643c10ad2.png)\n\nIntervals with one bracket and one parenthesis are called **half-open** (or **half-closed**)." :tutorial/title "Half-Open Intervals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060773}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059386 :question/id #uuid "b828f144-b8b8-5eda-94f1-43cccdcb1aaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10786" :question/problem "Write the interval $y\\in\\big(-4, \\sqrt{2}\\big]$ as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{\\big(}}} -4, \\sqrt2\\, {\\color{blue}{\\big]}}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,.$\n\n- The right endpoint $\\sqrt2$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq      }}\\,.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n-4 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt  }}\\,\\,   y \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,\\, \\sqrt 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Inequalities"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060774}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059387 :question/id #uuid "45d05c7d-e278-552f-a524-926d4a57d870" :question/math-academy-id "e-10787" :question/problem "What interval corresponds to the segment shown below?\n\n![](images/43/430481f9d0863b0bb5602f949e6f994d8007ea29fdd741a485d880df8d5d2dec.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}}  \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-2,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Number Lines"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059388 :question/id #uuid "6d4bd0a2-0961-54c1-b781-b470d420d848" :question/math-academy-id "e-10816" :question/problem "Express the inequality $1 \\le x \\le4$ in interval notation." :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $4$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4].\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Closed Intervals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060973 :tutorial/content "Now consider the region below, which represents the inequality $-3 \\leq x \\leq 1.$\n\n![](images/38/38c5bf5408bbbeb0f63e198e0564a37e8fe6a34d7f2ce340751d8000443123ed.png)\n\nWe express this inequality using interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-3,1]\n$$\n\nThe brackets $[\\phantom{0}]$ tell us that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=1$ *are* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval includes the endpoints, we say that it is **closed**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1].\n$$\n\nThe above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (closed) interval $[-3,1].$*\"" :tutorial/title "Closed Intervals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060771}}] :activity/title "Interval Notation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059917}] [#edb/ref 17592186050136 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060584} :activity/id #uuid "b1ce0b0f-b6c2-5cc2-9063-1be68a8f54d5" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060907 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given two vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 2,3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = \\langle -4,4 \\rangle\n$$\n\nshown below. How do we compute the vectors $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$ and $-5\\mathbf{a}$ in terms of the standard basis $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}$?\n\n![](images/25/254809cbc834b493759a6fa09222a2c90c5fbfc3280d22e82470e760a7a4c508.png)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$, we collect all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s. We know that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}3}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}-4}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}4}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= (2i + 3j) + (-4i + 4j) \\\\\n&= (2 + (-4))i + (3 + 4)j \\\\\n&=-2i + 7j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo compute $-5\\mathbf{a}$, we multiply each of the components by $-5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5 \\cdot a &= -5(2i + 3j) \\\\\n&= (-5 \\cdot 2)i + (-5 \\cdot 3)j \\\\\n&=-10i + - 15j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059523 :question/id #uuid "1d2717f9-b905-59eb-85d7-6db4f9c9dc7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4292" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$ if $\\mathbf{a}=-4\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=12\\mathbf{i}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 2b &= (-4i + 6j) - 2(12i) \\\\\n&=-4i + 6j - 24i \\\\\n&= (-4i - 24i) + 6j \\\\\n&=-28i + 6j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Addition and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors in Component Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060908 :tutorial/content "To add or subtract vectors given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to add or subtract each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨2, 5⟩ + ⟨- 3, 7⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨2 + (-3), 5 + 7⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= ⟨- 1, 12⟩ \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 + (-3) \\\\ 5 + 7\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, to multiply a vector given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to multiply each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5 \\cdot ⟨2, 5⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨(-5) \\cdot 2, (-5) \\cdot 5⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= ⟨- 10,-25⟩ \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}(-5) \\cdot 2 \\\\ (-5) \\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors in Angle Bracket or Column Vector Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060587}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059524 :question/id #uuid "2cb63c87-320a-5cb4-b413-8c6bf9723641" :question/math-academy-id "e-4293" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -7, 15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 14, -11 \\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 3b &= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - 3 \\cdot ⟨14,-11⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - ⟨42,-33⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7 - 42, 15 - (-33)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 49, 48⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors Using Angle Bracket Notation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059525 :question/id #uuid "43cfef07-255d-5167-b815-267883918993" :question/math-academy-id "e-4294" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ Find $2\\mathbf{a}-(\\mathbf{b}-5\\mathbf{c})$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a - (b - 5c) &= 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} - (\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} - 5 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} - (\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -31\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Column Vectors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060909 :tutorial/content "To denote a vector, we have used two notations:\n\n- a single bold lowercase letter, such as $\\mathbf{v}$, or\n- two uppercase letters representing the points of the ends of the vector, with an arrow over them, such as $\\overrightarrow{AB}$.\n\nWe will mostly stick to these notations. However, there are many possible notations for vectors. For example, sometimes a vector can be denoted as\n$\\vec{v}, \\quad \\overrightarrow{V}, \\quad v, \\quad \\hat{v}$.\nThe rightmost notation, $\\hat{v}$, is usually used to represent unit vectors in particular." :tutorial/title "Common Notations Used When Describing Vectors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060590}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059526 :question/id #uuid "0adf8fad-9a6f-5eb1-970f-11633ba98ca1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4295" :question/problem "The vectors $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ are given in the picture below. Find the coordinates of $\\vec{a}+6\\vec{b}$.\n\n![](images/56/56b10e759084da6fb21c8421bc9ff6d74aac2602df28f6c8e478bd874d7d6e9f.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\vec{a}=\\langle -3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\vec{b}=\\langle 4,5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, to compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^(→) + 6b^(→) &= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + 6 \\cdot ⟨4, 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨6 \\cdot 4, 6 \\cdot 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨24, 30⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨21, 32⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors Given in Alternative Notation"}}] :activity/title "Addition and Scalar Multiplication of Cartesian Vectors in 2D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059922}] [#edb/ref 17592186048252 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088517} :activity/id #uuid "79ff03a7-7063-5363-abbf-4b37f65e2992" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088890 :tutorial/content "Some important types of matrices have special names.\n\n- An $m \\times n$ matrix in which all entries are zeros is called a **zero matrix**. For example, the following matrices are both zero matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe use the notation $O_{m \\times n}$ to represent the zero-matrix with $m$ rows and $n$ columns. So, the zero matrices shown above are $O_{3 \\times 2}$ and $O_{2 \\times 2},$ respectively.\n\n- A $n \\times n$ matrix (meaning the number of rows equals the number of columns) is called a **square matrix**. For example, the following matrices are square matrices of dimensions $2\\times 2$ and $3\\times 3,$ respectively:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088518}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087695 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087502 :question/id #uuid "9c82456c-49ea-5a5e-b0a2-0ab17059acee" :question/math-academy-id "e-4390" :question/problem "Which of the following are zero matrices?\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $P$ is a zero matrix since *all* its entries are zeros.\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a zero matrix since some of its entries are not zeros (for instance, $q_{12}=0.1\\neq0$).\n\n- $R$ is a zero matrix since *all* its entries are zeros.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $R$ are zero matrices."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Zero Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087503 :question/id #uuid "bcb710d0-a687-5686-ae3a-0d0773857530" :question/math-academy-id "e-4391" :question/problem "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $U$ is *not* a square matrix since the numbers of rows and columns are not the same. It has $2$ rows and $3$ columns, so it's a $2 \\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- $W$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $3$ rows and $3$ columns, so it is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- $Z$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $2$ rows and $2$ columns, so it is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, $W$ and $Z$ are square matrices."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Square Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087504 :question/id #uuid "79974103-2da9-599a-b33b-abac3d32613a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4392" :question/problem "Which of the following are diagonal matrices?\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $Q$ is a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. Also, all the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are zeros.\n\nNotice that $Q$ is not an identity matrix since the diagonal entries are not all ones.\n\n- $R$ is *not* a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. But some of the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are not zeros (for instance, $r_{13}=1 \\neq 0$).\n\n- $S$ is a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. Also, all the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are zeros.\n\nNotice that $S$ is not an identity matrix since the diagonal entries are not all ones.\n\nTherefore, only $Q$ and $S$ are diagonal matrices."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Special Square Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088521}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087698 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087505 :question/id #uuid "0e3ad3cd-f7c4-55f4-8a0b-b850283bc6b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4393" :question/problem "How can we best describe the following matrix?\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $F$ is a matrix that has a single column. Therefore, it is a column vector."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Other Matrices"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088892 :tutorial/content "There are some special types of square matrices.\n\n- A square matrix is called **diagonal**, if all entries that do not lie on the main diagonal (from top-left to bottom-right) are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all diagonal matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal.\n\n- An **identity matrix** is a diagonal matrix where the entries on the diagonal are all $1$'s (and all other entries are $0$'s). For example, the following matrices are all identity matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\,1\\,\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe use the notation $I_n$ for the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFinally, as we've seen before:\n\n- If a matrix has only one row, then it's called a **row vector**. An example of a row vector is shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- If a matrix has only one column, then it's called a **column vector**. An example of a column vector is shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Diagonal, Identity, and Other Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088520}}] :activity/title "Zero, Square, Diagonal and Identity Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087929}]}
